Fcheckd
-------

Fchekd is an inode monitoring daemon based on inotify which allows its users to 
monitor files and directories based on supplied config and execute user scripts
on specified events with monitored files.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Building from source

Cargo and rustc are build dependencies. Firstly:

git clone https://codeberg.org/subparr/fcheckd.git
cd fcheckd

Then you can either "cargo build --release" to only get the artifact in 
target/release/ or run install/install.sh, which will build and install fcheckd. 
Currently only systemd and openrc are supported.

Some parts of installation script require root priveleges so make sure to check 
out the sources.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Basic usage

Available events are specified by inotify api and listed in its manpage, i.e. 
see inotify(7).

Config format as follows:

#specifies a single watch, there can be several in config
[watch]]									
#path to watched file or directory
path = "/absolute/path/to/watched/file"	                                   
#optional, ignored if path leads to file
recursive = true								
#path to script that will be executed
script = "/absolute/path/to/script"						
#events with file(s) on which script will be executed
events = ["close", "modify"]						   

Adding a watch to a directory means adding watch to all the files contained in
this directory.
Do note that to avoid ambiguity paths MUST be absolute.

If original watch path points at a symlink it WILL be resolved, although no
further symlinks will be resolved in case of recursive = true.
This was made intentionally to avoid symlink loops.

Default config locations are /etc/fcheckd/config.toml and
$HOME/.config/fcheckd/config.toml. Custom configlocation can be specified with
the --config flag.
Check fcheckd --help for full list of flags.

For config reload send SIGHUP(1) to the daemon or simply
"systemctl reload fchekd" in case you use systemd.
For openrc it is "rc-service fcheckd reload".
Do note that config gets reloaded indefinitely whether it was changed or not.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Known quirks

I strongly suggest to test watches by specifying dummy script first as the
circumstances under which the watches are triggered might not be so obvious.
For instance, IN_ACCESS is triggered for each read() and uponusing fish shell
autosuggestions inside a watched directory, watch will get triggered on each
keystroke as a filename is typed in, as read() is called on each keystroke.

One possible way of mitigating a watch firing several times when a directory or 
a file have been accessed by something is to specify "oneshot" event.
This way, watch can only be triggered once and theninvalidated by the kernel. 
Do note that if another event somehow precedes invalidation event it will still
beprocessed.

I also suggest to be very careful with your scripts, as you need to make sure
that they won't trigger your watches, which in return will trigger the same
scripts... You get the point, recursively triggering watches
is possible and not recommended, although is going to be fixed with the delay
config parameter.

In current version watches for files are established as pointing to files. It is
important because some text editors(like neovim) upon editing a file substitute
the original with an edited copy, which means the original file gets deleted
and the watch gets removed by the kernel, since there is nothing to watch on the
original inode, which also means IN_MODIFY (or "modify" in config) flag doesn't
even get triggered upon file "modification". That's why it is important to
distinguish the tool being an inode watcher rather than a file watcher, until
the file watching logic gets changed.

For future updates that means that you as a user will still need to be aware of 
such quirks when it comes to monitoring files or directories, although your
watches will be safe, unless it doesn't depend on my code.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Security considerations

No isolation and/or limiting techniques were used in service files except for
memory and child process limit, reason being that this way tool will not assume
the use case as, for instance, introducing namespace isolation like private tmp
will limit the watching capabilites of the daemon. Intdroducing seccomp will
limit the capabilities of your scripts (same case with namespace isolation),
and MAC is not of my concern as a developer of the tool since it is user
controlled and policies may depend on the use case.

Configuration is read in the following order:
Cli config path via -c arg -> ~/.config/fcheckd/config.toml ->
/etc/fcheckd/config.toml

Default permissions for /etc/fcheckd/config.toml are 600 root owned and this 
configuration is prefferable whenrunning daemon from root. The configuration
should NOT be accessible for non root users as changing the config for daemon
run from root introduces whatever the non root user may come up with.

Always make sure that for daemon running from root the config, if passed via
cli options, is unaccessible tonon root users the same way as config under
/etc/fcheckd/ is unaccessible.

For daemon running from unpriveleged user home config location is prefferable
and is pretty much a default as evident from configuration load order.
